From a84e1c7da4223ec63cc3a5346daee109966adc5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 23:22:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10217/11362] Renumber my REQ-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-3 to -4: the
 identifier was already taken

I appended a requirement under REQ-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-3 on 2026-08-28 (commit
080baaadff) without checking whether the identifier was free. It was not.
REQ-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-3 was taken on 2026-08-23 (b54a7aa3e6) by "A
Success-Shaped Verdict String SHALL NOT Declare Itself Partial" -- a
requirement about the SAME four artifacts mine addresses, exp6506 among them.
Two different requirements shared one identifier for a day, so every trace to
it was ambiguous.

Renumbered mine to -4, with an append-only correction note in the spec saying
what happened and why. Neither section is deleted; the 2026-08-23 -3 is
untouched.

The two are complementary and the note says so. -3 forbids the CONTRADICTION --
a `complete_`-prefixed verdict string declaring itself partial. -4 answers what
-3 leaves open: if not partial, then what? Choose by whether the RUN finished,
and give the mixed-result case `null`. That is why the seven writers could be
corrected rather than only flagged.

39 references across 18 files follow the rename. Two were deliberately NOT
renamed because they genuinely mean the original -3:
scripts/adversarial_verify.py:3856 ("a success-shaped verdict string that
declares itself partial") and tests/python/test_verdict_prefix_class_
contradiction.py. Every reference was read before deciding, not swept -- the
"finished ... declares null, not partial" family is mine; the contradiction
family is not.

Found while verifying a teammate's claim that the spec had no resurrected
duplicate heading. It did have two headings, which was true and benign as
stated, and the real defect underneath was mine. 55 tests green.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
